CAT = "aceScreening"; SEG = "SCR"
Q = [
('A new client answers yes to a PAR-Q+ item about chest pain during activity. What comes next?',
 'Follow-up pages or clearance', ['Start at low intensity anyway', 'Run a maximal treadmill test first', 'Give a heart-healthy diet plan'],
 'A yes answer on the PAR-Q+ leads to its follow-up pages or to medical clearance before training starts. A trainer does not test or train through a possible cardiac symptom.'),
('Under the ACSM preparticipation screening algorithm, which factor sorts clients first?',
 'Current exercise habits', ['Body mass index category', 'Resting heart rate level', 'Number of risk factors'],
 'The ACSM algorithm starts with whether the person exercises regularly now, then known disease, then signs or symptoms. Counting risk factors is no longer the deciding step.'),
('Which finding counts as a positive family-history risk factor for cardiovascular disease?',
 'Father had a heart attack at 50', ['Uncle had a stroke at 70', 'Mother had high cholesterol', 'Grandfather had type 2 diabetes'],
 'Family history counts when a father or other first-degree male relative has a cardiac event before 55, or a mother or first-degree female relative before 65. A grandfather or uncle is not first-degree.'),
('A client quit smoking four months ago. How is smoking scored as a risk factor?',
 'Still counts as positive', ['No longer counts at all', 'Counts as a negative factor', 'Counts only after a year'],
 'Smoking counts for current smokers and for those who quit within the past six months. Four months is inside that window.'),
('Which value is a negative (protective) risk factor for heart disease?',
 'HDL of 60 mg/dL or higher', ['LDL of 60 mg/dL or lower', 'Total cholesterol under 240', 'Fasting glucose under 125'],
 'An HDL cholesterol of 60 mg/dL or more offsets one risk factor. Low LDL is healthy but is not the defined negative risk factor.'),
('A client takes a beta blocker. How should exercise intensity be monitored?',
 'Rating of perceived exertion', ['Age-predicted heart rate zones', 'Karvonen heart rate targets', 'Peak heart rate from a watch'],
 'Beta blockers blunt the heart-rate response at rest and during exercise, so heart-rate targets from formulas overstate effort. RPE or the talk test tracks intensity instead.'),
('Which medication class most often raises the risk of dehydration and electrolyte loss?',
 'Diuretics', ['Statins', 'Antidepressants', 'ACE inhibitors'],
 'Diuretics increase urine output, which can lower fluid and potassium levels. Clients on them need attention to hydration, especially in heat.'),
('During the first interview, a client keeps arms crossed and gives one-word answers. Best response?',
 'Ask open-ended questions', ['Move on to the fitness tests', 'List your credentials again', 'Point out the body language'],
 'Open-ended questions invite the client to talk and build rapport. Rushing to testing or naming the posture can make a guarded client more defensive.'),
('A client says, "I know I should exercise, and I might start in a few months." Which stage fits?',
 'Contemplation', ['Precontemplation', 'Preparation', 'Action'],
 'In contemplation a person intends to change within the next six months but has not started. Precontemplation means no intention to change in the next six months.'),
('A client has walked three days a week for two months. Which stage of change is this?',
 'Action', ['Maintenance', 'Preparation', 'Contemplation'],
 'Action covers the first six months of regular behavior. Maintenance begins once it has been sustained for more than six months.'),
('Which tool weighs the pros and cons a client sees in changing a behavior?',
 'Decisional balance', ['Self-efficacy scale', 'Exercise history form', 'Readiness checklist'],
 'Decisional balance lists perceived benefits and costs of change. As people move toward action, the pros come to outweigh the cons.'),
('In the health belief model, a client who thinks heart disease "won\'t happen to me" lacks:',
 'Perceived susceptibility', ['Perceived severity', 'Cues to take action', 'Perceived self-efficacy'],
 'Perceived susceptibility is the belief that one could get the condition. Severity is how serious the person thinks it would be.'),
('Which goal meets all the SMART criteria?',
 'Walk 30 minutes, 4 days a week, 8 weeks', ['Walk 30 minutes as often as possible', 'Run a marathon in two weeks from zero', 'Walk 4 days a week, starting soon'],
 'A SMART goal is specific, measurable, attainable, relevant and time-bound. Only the walking goal names the action, amount and timeframe.'),
('What does the "R" stand for in the GROW coaching model?',
 'Reality', ['Resources', 'Results', 'Readiness'],
 'GROW stands for goal, reality, options and will. Reality explores where the client is now before options are weighed.'),
('In motivational interviewing, what is a reflection?',
 'Restating what the client said', ['Asking a closed yes/no question', 'Giving advice on the next step', 'Praising effort after a session'],
 'A reflection repeats or rephrases the client\'s words, which shows listening and invites more detail. It is the R in OARS.'),
('A client lists time, cost and a busy job as reasons not to train. These are best called:',
 'Exercise barriers', ['Contraindications', 'Cardiac risk factors', 'Outcome goals'],
 'Lack of time, cost and work demands are common barriers that a trainer helps the client plan around. A contraindication is a medical reason not to do an exercise.'),
('Which dietary evaluation method is within a personal trainer\'s scope?',
 'Reviewing a food log', ['Prescribing a meal plan', 'Ordering a blood panel', 'Diagnosing a deficiency'],
 'Trainers may review food logs or recalls to give general guidance. Meal plans for medical needs, lab tests and diagnosis belong to dietitians and physicians.'),
('A client with type 2 diabetes reports frequent numbness in the feet. What should the trainer do?',
 'Inspect skin, pick low-impact work', ['Avoid all weight-bearing exercise now', 'Add barefoot balance drills', 'Raise intensity to lower glucose'],
 'Peripheral neuropathy raises the risk of unnoticed foot injury, so clients check their feet and favor proper footwear and lower-impact options. Weight-bearing work is not banned outright.'),
('Which prenatal client needs to stop exercising and contact her provider?',
 'Fluid leaking from the vagina', ['Mild breathlessness on stairs', 'Wanting to keep strength work', 'Feeling warm after a walk'],
 'ACOG lists amniotic fluid leakage, vaginal bleeding, regular painful contractions, chest pain and dizziness as reasons to stop. Mild breathlessness and warmth are normal.'),
('Which response shows a trainer adapting the interview for a client with low health literacy?',
 'Plain words, then check understanding', ['Hand over the full intake packet first', 'Use exact clinical terminology', 'Skip the health history entirely'],
 'Plain language and teach-back confirm the client understood. Dense forms or jargon can hide misunderstandings, and the health history is never skipped.'),
]
